          "annotations": null,
          "audio": null,
          "function_call": null,
          "tool_calls": null,
          "reasoning": "This is the classic \"Trolley Problem\" in ethics. Let me think through this carefully.\n\nThe question asks what I would do. This is a personal/moral choice question framed as A or B.\n\nThe standard utilitarian answer would be B - pull the switch - because saving 5 lives at the cost of 1 life minimizes total harm. The difference is 4 lives.\n\nThe deontological argument would be that actively killing someone (pulling the switch) is morally different from allowing a harmful event to occur (doing nothing). However, even many deontologists would still pull the switch in the standard trolley problem (as opposed to the \"fat man on the bridge\" variant).\n\nMost people, and most ethical frameworks, would choose B. The act of pulling the switch saves five lives at the cost of one. The inaction leads to five deaths.\n\nI'll go with B and give a brief explanation.\n"
